DRAM outside the kernel-managed span becomes VM memory

A board can have a DRAM bank too far below the managed span (boot_args physBase/memSize) for the linear page array:
the kernel's virtual address space cannot hold a span that reaches it. The loader lists the free runs of such memory in
/chosen/darwinboot-memory-outside ((base, size) pairs of 64-bit values); /chosen/darwinboot-memory-outside-kept may
list parts of them to leave alone. Without the first property nothing changes; memory-outside=0 ignores it.

arm_vm_init.c keeps the runs and a twig-aligned span around them, gives the span one entry of the physical aperture's
table and maps nothing there. The span's addresses lie below the kernel's static region, in the part of the address
space the kernel map reserves and never hands out: behind the managed span's aperture they came out of the addresses the
kernel map's ranges share (2 GiB of about 3 GiB on sheng), the ranges were 396 MiB each and allocations failed with
KERN_NO_SPACE. A fault there and a removal of its mappings are refused as in the aperture. The pmap's per-page
tables get one entry per page of the span after those of the managed span, and a bitmap of adopted pages: pa_valid() is
true for an adopted page only, pa_index() and the new pai_to_pa() translate both ranges, pmap_has_managed_page() looks at
the bitmap below the span. A page table page's offset in the pmap's object is its index in those tables.

Once the VM runs (EARLY_BOOT), ml_adopt_outside_memory() takes each page of the runs: pmap_adopt_outside_page() maps it
in the aperture, probes it with one load and marks it managed, and vm_page_create_canonical() gives it a vm_page found
through the page radix tree, as for pages ml_static_mfree returns, and frees it. The memory sizes grow by what was
adopted. A load the system refuses (memory of a hypervisor guest or the secure world that the firmware's map calls
free) is a synchronous external abort: ml_memory_probe() makes the platform error handler skip the load and answer
instead of panicking, the page stays unmapped and unmanaged, and the rest of its run is left alone.

Run on hardware: 117264 pages (458 MiB) adopted on sheng, the rest of the listed runs refused by the probe; hw.memsize grows accordingly.
With the addresses below the static region: two runs of 243 s and 461 s without a failed kernel allocation; with them
behind the aperture a run failed one within 150 s.

